WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.049
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:10.210
What could be a more delightful way to celebrate
new year's eve than reading about new breakthroughs

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.120
in machine learning research!
Let's talk about an excellent new paper from

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.959
the Google DeepMind guys.

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:21.410
In machine learning, we usually have a set
of problems for which we are looking for solutions.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:25.980
For instance, here is an image, please tell
me what is seen on it. Here is a computer

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.040
game, please beat level three. One problem,
one solution.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:35.460
In this case, we are not looking for one solution,
we are looking for a computer program, an

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.600
algorithm, that can solve any number of problems
of the same kind.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.690
This work is based on a recurrent neural network,
which we discussed in a previous episode - in

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:50.260
short, it means that it tries to learn not
one something but a sequence of things, and

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:56.390
in this example, it learns to add two large
numbers together. As a big number can be imagined

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:01.589
as a sequence of digits, this can be done
through a sequence of operations - it first

00:01:01.589 --> 00:01:06.650
reads the two input numbers and then carries
out the addition, keeps track of carrying

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.620
digits, and goes on to the next digit. On
the right, you can see the individual commands

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:17.690
executed in the computer program it came up
with.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:22.560
It can also learn how to rotate images of
different cars around to obtain a frontal

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:32.060
pose. This is also a sequence of rotation
actions until the desired output is reached.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.610
Learning more rudimentary sorting algorithms
to put the numbers in ascending order is also

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.000
possible.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.100
One key difference between recurrent neural networks and this is that these neural programmer interpreters

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.580
are able to generalize better. What does this
mean? This means that if the technique can

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:55.230
learn from someone how to sort a set of 20
numbers, it can generalize its knowledge to

00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:01.360
much longer sequences. So it essentially tries
to learn the algorithm behind sorting from

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.040
a few examples. Previous techniques were unable
to achieve this, and, as we can see, it can

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.239
deal with a variety of problems. I am absolutely
spellbound by this kind of learning, because

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:19.030
it really behaves like a novice human user
would: looking at what experts do and trying

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.379
to learn and understand the logic behind their
actions.

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:29.349
Happy New Year to all of you Fellow Scholars!
May it be ample in joy and beautiful papers,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:34.629
may our knowledge grow according to Moore's
law. And of course, may the force be with

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:35.319
you.

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:38.939
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next year!

